> I _thought_ I had set that as a required dependency in the package, but 
> perhaps I got that wrong (my experience building debs is not great). Or 
> does "dpkg -i" ignore deps?

Yes and no. It doesn't resolve dependencies, but complains if
dependencies aren't fulfilled. Your package says

| Depends: libc6, libncursesw5

which my system fulfills. But apparently, Debian stable either doesn't
ship a version recent enough or Debian uses other compile time settings.
It would probably a good ide to use a versioned dependency, but I cannot
tell which version you actually need.

I tried the same package on Debian unstable. It works there with this
version:

$ apt-cache policy libncursesw5
libncursesw5:
  Installed: 5.9-4
  Candidate: 5.9-4
  Version table:
 *** 5.9-4 0
        500 http://ftp.de.debian.org/debian/ testing/main amd64 Packages
        500 http://ftp.de.debian.org/debian/ sid/main amd64 Packages
        100 /var/lib/dpkg/status

Oh, and there's a separate library libtinfo5 as well:

$ $ apt-cache policy libtinfo5
libtinfo5:
  Installed: 5.9-4
  Candidate: 5.9-4
  Version table:
 *** 5.9-4 0
        500 http://ftp.de.debian.org/debian/ testing/main amd64 Packages
        500 http://ftp.de.debian.org/debian/ sid/main amd64 Packages
        100 /var/lib/dpkg/status

In Debian unstable, libncursesw5 v5.9-4 has a dependency in libtinfo5
v5.9-4, so adding libtinfo5 as dependency is superfluous as long as
nitrohack depends on a recent enough libncursesw5.

